Found by the fidelity-auditor during the third k108 review; not caused by k108, HEAD 04e3a23 behaves the same.

Case (callback form of render, memory loader): main.njk = A{% for n in ['i.njk', 'bad.njk'] %}{% include n %}{% endfor %}B ; i.njk = i ; bad.njk = {% if %} ; env.render('main.njk', {}, cb).
Upstream v3.2.4: cb(null, 'AiB'), then cb(error '(main.njk) Template render error: (bad.njk) [Line 1, Column 7] unexpected token: %}').
Port: cb(error) only, same text.

Cause: upstream's Template render hands a compile error to callbackAsap (src/environment.js line 466), so the error arrives after the current turn; the plain for does not wait for its includes, the root finishes and delivers the output first. The port's _callback_asap is synchronous (ADR 0003), the error comes first and Template render then drops the output (did_error guard). The same should hold for the other callbackAsap site that takes an error (Environment render when get_template fails, line 310) wherever synchronous code still runs after it -- not measured.

ADR 0003 records that callbacks fire synchronously, not this consequence; DIFFERENCES in lib/Text/Nunjucks.pm does not list it and no test pins it. Options: record it under ADR 0003 and DIFFERENCES with a pin generated from real upstream (the oracle cannot take a callback; maint/include-failure-fixtures can), or defer the error until the current synchronous run has unwound. The second is a design question for the maintainer. Related: k104, k119.

2026-11-17 11:58 fidelity-auditor: the case in the body is measured as written (upstream two calls, tree and HEAD one). Control measured too: the same loop with a template that does not exist in place of bad.njk equals upstream (one call, 'template not found') -- only the compile-error path differs.
